entry.summary:
Most uptime monitors tell you your API is down. Mine tells you why. Most monitors do something like this: const response = await axios.get(url);
That tells you nothing. You know the request failed. You don't know where. Switching to native http with timing hooks const req = transport.request(options, (res) => {
req.on('socket', (socket) => {
Now every ping stores dns_lookup_ms, tcp_connect_ms, tls_handshake_ms, and ttfb_ms separately in the database alongside the usual status_code and response_time_ms The inference logic // DNS spiked but TCP was fine — DNS issue
// TCP failed entirely — server unreachable
// Everything fine until TTFB — server-side problem
// Status code tells us exactly what happened
No ML, no black box. Just rule-based inference against a baseline. A 503 with normal DNS/TCP/TLS timings but a spiked TTFB looks completely different from a connection timeout with no TCP at all. What it looks like in practice When a monitor goes down, instead of just logging the failure, Pulse shows: Root Cause Analysis DNS Lookup → 34ms normal
TLS Handshake → 71ms normal Suggestion: Server is responding but very slowly — The baseline problem What I learned Pulse is free — 5 monitors, no credit card. If you want to see the root cause analysis in action or poke around the implementation: Pulse Happy to hear opinions!
